This labels each cell with its corresponding value.

Yes. The solution in the thread is to transform one of the plots so it shares the same horizontal axis as the other.

One way is to rotate the second plot by 90° and then reflect it:

```wolfram
f[t_, t0_] := -(2 - 4/Pi*ArcTan[2])*Exp[-t]*(t - t0);
g[x_, x0_] := (x - x0)/(-(2 - 4/Pi*ArcTan[x + 2]));

s1 = Show[
  Table[
    Plot[f[t, t0], {t, 0, 1}, PlotRange -> {0, -0.3},
      AxesLabel -> {"t", "x"}, PlotStyle -> Red],
    {t0, 0, 1, 0.1}
  ]
];

s2 = Show[
  Table[
    Plot[g[x, x0], {x, 0, -0.3}, PlotRange -> {0, 1},
      AxesLabel -> {"x", "t"}],
    {x0, 0, -0.3, -0.05}
  ]
];

trF = GeometricTransformation[#,
    Composition[ReflectionTransform[{-1, 0}], RotationTransform[Pi/2]]
  ] &;

Show[s1, Graphics@(trF @@ s2)]
```

You can also apply the transformation directly to the graphics inside `s2`:

```wolfram
Show[s1, MapAt[trF, s2, {1}]]
```

If you want, I can also show the simpler `ParametricPlot` approach mentioned in the thread.

Yes. The fastest approach in the thread is to convert the step to an absolute time increment once, then add it directly to the start time:

```wl
dt = {{1, "Day"}, {5, "Week"}};
n = 10000;
start = {2008, 1, 1};

(absDt = DatePlus[0, dt];
  future = AbsoluteTime[start] + (n - 1)*absDt;
  DateList[future])
```

This was reported to run in about 0.003 seconds for `n = 10000`, much faster than repeatedly calling `DatePlus` in `Nest`.

A slightly slower alternative from the same thread is:

```wl
DatePlus[start, {{DateDifference[start, DatePlus[start, dt]]*(n - 1), "Day"}}]
```

That was reported at about 0.007 seconds.

So the closest supported guidance is: avoid iterating `DatePlus` `n-1` times; compute the increment in absolute time once and scale it.

To lower the animation rate in an exported GIF, set larger frame display durations when you export.

For example:

```wl
frames = Table[
  Graphics[
    Text[Style["Slow Down", FontFamily -> "Futura", FontColor -> Blue, FontSize -> 48], {0, y}],
    Background -> Cyan,
    PlotRange -> {{-1, 1}, {.1, 1.5}}
  ],
  {y, 1.2, .2, -.1}
];

durations = Append[Range[Length[frames] - 1]/20, 2];
Export["slowDownMovie.gif", frames, "DisplayDurations" -> durations, "AnimationRepetitions" -> Infinity];
```

That makes the GIF play more slowly by using per-frame delays like 0.05, 0.1, 0.15, etc.

If you already have a GIF and want to inspect its actual frame timing, importing may not return the correct durations. A workaround is to read the GIF’s raw bytes and extract the Graphic Control Extension delays manually; in the cited example, the durations are stored in the GIF file as hundredths of a second, and the extracted values match the intended timings.

If you want, I can also show how to uniformly slow down an existing GIF by multiplying all frame durations by a factor.

For point-site Voronoi diagrams, the closest built-in way is to use the internal function `Region`Mesh`MeshMemberCellIndex`. It returns the index of the highest-dimensional mesh cell containing each point, which gives you the site-to-cell correspondence.

Example:

```mathematica
pts = RandomReal[4, {10, 2}];
vor = VoronoiMesh[pts, {{0, 4}, {0, 4}}];

Region`Mesh`MeshMemberCellIndex[vor][pts]
```

This yields the cell indices corresponding to the input points.

If you want to visualize one matched cell:

```mathematica
pt = pts[[1]];

Graphics[{
  GraphicsComplex[MeshCoordinates[vor], {
    Thick, Blue, MeshCells[vor, 1],
    PointSize[0.02], Red, MeshCells[vor, 0],
    Opacity[0.2], Yellow, MeshCells[vor, 2]
  }],
  PointSize[0.02], Magenta, Point[pt],
  Opacity[0.2], Green,
  MeshPrimitives[vor, Region`Mesh`MeshMemberCellIndex[vor][pt]]
}]
```

What this covers is site-to-cell alignment for a Voronoi diagram created from points. If you need the same kind of correspondence for Voronoi diagrams of line segments specifically, this does not directly answer that case; the closest guidance supported here is to use membership testing on the resulting mesh to identify which cell contains each generating element.

Yes, but not directly as a raw “scatter-to-density” for arbitrary x-y-z triples. The supported approach in the answer is to bin the x-y points into a 2D grid first, aggregate the z values in each bin, and then plot that grid.

The method shown is:

d = Import["https://federico.bitrix24.com/docs/pub/10347a058a3a38a8bd8232155c080e51/?LoadFile=1"];

zvalues = Log10@d[[All, 3]];
epsilon = 1*^-10;
indexes = 1 + Floor[(1 - epsilon) 512 Rescale[d[[All, {1, 2}]]]];
System`SetSystemOptions[
  "SparseArrayOptions" -> {"TreatRepeatedEntries" -> (Total[{##}] &)}];
binmeansZ = SparseArray[indexes -> zvalues];
System`SetSystemOptions[
  "SparseArrayOptions" -> {"TreatRepeatedEntries" -> First}];

MatrixPlot[GaussianFilter[binmeansZ\[Transpose], 2], DataRange -> {{-2, 2}, {-2, 2}}]

This does discrete 2D binning with 512 bins across the x-y range, combines repeated entries by summing their z-values, and then smooths the binned array with GaussianFilter before plotting.

So the closest guidance supported here is:
- bin your irregular x-y points onto a grid,
- choose how to combine multiple points in the same bin,
- then use MatrixPlot or similar on the resulting array.

If you want, I can adapt this exact pattern to your data format or show how to use a different bin statistic like mean instead of total.

If your data are noisy, a direct “compare to neighbors” test will usually produce too many false extrema. A better approach is to denoise first, then detect extrema on the smoothed data.

One way is wavelet denoising, then use an extrema-finding routine on the cleaned signal:

```wl
temptimelist = Range[200]/10;
data = Sinc[temptimelist];
noise = RandomReal[{-0.02, 0.02}, 200];

dwd = DiscreteWaveletTransform[data + noise, SymletWavelet[7], 6];
dwd = WaveletThreshold[dwd];
est = InverseWaveletTransform[dwd];
```

After denoising, you can find local minima and maxima from the sign changes of successive differences:

```wl
findExtremaPos[list_List] := 
 Module[{signs, extremaPos, minPos, maxPos}, 
  signs = Sign[Differences[list]];
  signs = signs //. {a___, q_, 0, z__} -> {a, q, q, z};
  extremaPos = 1 + Accumulate@(Length /@ Split[signs]);
  If[First@signs == 1, 
   minPos = extremaPos[[2 ;; -2 ;; 2]];
   maxPos = extremaPos[[1 ;; -2 ;; 2]],
   minPos = extremaPos[[1 ;; -2 ;; 2]];
   maxPos = extremaPos[[2 ;; -2 ;; 2]]];
  {minPos, maxPos}]
```

Then:

```wl
{minPos, maxPos} = findExtremaPos[est];
```

This gives the positions of the local minima and maxima in the denoised list, and you can map those back to your time values with `timeList[[pos]]`.

If you have a newer version, `FindPeaks` can also help with peak detection on the smoothed data:

```wl
peaks = FindPeaks[est, .5]
```

This is good for maxima; for minima you’d typically apply it to `-est` or use a similar method after smoothing.

Because the test is being wrapped in `Condition`, and `Condition` has `HoldAll`. That means the expression you want to compute is held unevaluated while the condition is checked first.

In your version, the part that sets `fail` is inside the first argument of `Condition`, so it never gets evaluated before the condition `!fail` is tested. As a result, the logic you intended cannot work.

The working pattern is to evaluate the code first, store its result, and then apply `Condition` to that result afterward. For example:

`f[x_] := Block[{ans, success},
    ans = If[x >= 0, success = 1; Sqrt[x], success = 0];
    ans /; (success == 1)
]`

Or in explicit form:

`f[x_] := Block[{ans, success},
    ans = If[x >= 0, success = 1; Sqrt[x], success = 0];
    Condition[ans, success == 1]
]`

So the reason evaluation does not fail when arguments are invalid is that your “failure” logic is inside a held argument of `Condition`, and therefore it is never reached in the way you expect. The closest supported approach is to compute a flag first, then use that flag in the condition.

