You can make the central 3D histogram with `Histogram3D`, then make the two side histograms separately with `Histogram` and place them as textures on flat polygons in 3D space.

A workable pattern is:

- build the 3D histogram from the 2D points
- build one histogram for each coordinate
- texture-map those histograms onto the side walls
- combine everything with `Show`

For example:

```wolfram
data = RandomVariate[
  MultinormalDistribution[{0, 0}, {{1, 0.9}, {0.9, 2}}],
  10^4
];

lightSources = {
  {"Directional", White, Scaled[{1, 0, 1}]},
  {"Directional", White, Scaled[{1, .5, 1}]},
  {"Directional", White, Scaled[{.5, 0, 1}]}
};

G1 = Histogram3D[
  data, {0.25}, "PDF",
  ColorFunction -> "Rainbow",
  PlotRange -> {{-4, 4}, {-4, 4}, All},
  ImageSize -> Medium,
  Boxed -> False,
  Lighting -> lightSources
];

G3 = Histogram[
  Transpose[data][[1]], {-4, 4, .25}, "Probability",
  ColorFunction -> Function[{height}, ColorData["Rainbow"][height]],
  Axes -> False
];

G4 = Histogram[
  Transpose[data][[2]], {-4, 4, .25}, "Probability",
  ColorFunction -> Function[{height}, ColorData["Rainbow"][height]],
  Axes -> False
];

G5 = Graphics3D[{
   EdgeForm[],
   Texture[G3],
   Polygon[{{4, 4, 0}, {-4, 4, 0}, {-4, 4, 0.2}, {4, 4, 0.2}},
    VertexTextureCoordinates -> {{0, 0}, {1, 0}, {1, 1}, {0, 1}}]
   },
  Axes -> False, Boxed -> False
];

G6 = Graphics3D[{
   EdgeForm[],
   Texture[G4],
   Polygon[{{-4, 4, 0}, {-4, -4, 0}, {-4, -4, 0.2}, {-4, 4, 0.2}},
    VertexTextureCoordinates -> {{0, 0}, {1, 0}, {1, 1}, {0, 1}}]
   },
  Axes -> False, Boxed -> False
];

Show[G1, G5, G6]
```

If you want a strict 2D representation instead, the closest supported option is to use `DensityHistogram` for the data, but that won’t give the side histograms integrated into a 3D scene.

