Yes. You can solve for the real and imaginary parts at the same time by separating the expression into `Re[...]` and `Im[...]`, then solving for `A` and `B`.

A pattern like this works:

```mathematica
ComplexExpand[
  Solve[
    {
      Re[(1 - Sqrt[x - I y])/(1 + Sqrt[x - I y])] == A,
      Im[(1 - Sqrt[x - I y])/(1 + Sqrt[x - I y])] == B
    },
    {A, B}
  ],
  TargetFunctions -> {Re, Im}
]
```

That returns rules for both `A` and `B` in terms of `x` and `y`.

If your goal is just to extract the real and imaginary parts of the whole expression, this same approach is the relevant one: use `Re` and `Im` on the expression, and `ComplexExpand` if you want Mathematica to rewrite them in terms of real variables.

