This happens because after the package is loaded once, `Test1`` gets put on `$ContextPath`.

Then when the package is run again, the unqualified name `function1` inside the private section is no longer treated as a fresh private symbol. It gets resolved to the already existing `Test1`function1`, so the definition ends up on that public symbol instead of `Test1`Private`function1`.

What your example is showing is:

- first run: `function1[x_] := x` creates `Test1`Private`function1`
- later runs: `function1` is found on the context path and is parsed as `Test1`function1`

To avoid this, either write the full private context explicitly:

```mathematica
BeginPackage["Test1`"];
Begin["`Private`"];
Test1`Private`function1[x_] := x
End[];
EndPackage[];
```

or clear the conflicting symbol before defining it:

```mathematica
BeginPackage["Test1`"];
Remove[function1];
Begin["`Private`"];
function1[x_] := x
End[];
EndPackage[];
```

If you want to reset the package context completely each time, you can also remove all symbols in `Test1`` before reloading it, for example with `Remove["Test1`*"]`.

Use `ShowLegend` rather than trying to attach `PlotLegends` directly to `Show`. The issue described is that `PlotLegends` can mess up graphics when combined with `Show`, while `ShowLegend` is intended for that use.

A working pattern is:

```mathematica
eqns = {Sin[3 Tanh[#]], Sin[Erf[#]]} &;
colors = {Blue, Red};
legends = TraditionalForm /@ eqns[x];

plot1 = Plot[Evaluate@eqns[x], {x, 0, π}, PlotStyle -> colors,
  Filling -> {1 -> {{2}, LightGreen}}];

plot2 = ListPlot[
  Select[RandomReal[{0, Pi}, {1000, 2}],
    IntervalMemberQ[Interval[Sort[eqns[First@#]]], Last@#] &
  ],
  PlotStyle -> Darker@Green
];

ShowLegend[
  Show[plot1, plot2],
  {{Graphics[{#1, Thick, Line[{{0, 0}, {1, 0}}]}], #2} & @@@
    Transpose[{colors, legends}],
   LegendPosition -> {-0.65, -0.5},
   LegendSpacing -> 0,
   LegendShadow -> None,
   LegendSize -> 0.6}
]
```

So the closest guidance is: build the plot with `Show`, then wrap the result with `ShowLegend` to add the legend.

One workable approach is to first smooth/denoise the data, then find extrema on the cleaned list.

The example solution uses wavelet denoising:

```wolfram
temptimelist = Range[200]/10;
data = Sinc[temptimelist];
noise = RandomReal[{-0.02, 0.02}, 200];

dwd = DiscreteWaveletTransform[data + noise, SymletWavelet[7], 6];
dwd = WaveletThreshold[dwd];
est = InverseWaveletTransform[dwd];
```

After that, extrema can be found from sign changes in the differences:

```wolfram
findExtremaPos[list_List] := 
 Module[{signs, extremaPos, minPos, maxPos},
  signs = Sign[Differences[list]];
  signs = signs //. {a___, q_, 0, z__} -> {a, q, q, z};
  extremaPos = 1 + Accumulate@(Length /@ Split[signs]);
  If[First@signs == 1,
   minPos = extremaPos[[2 ;; -2 ;; 2]];
   maxPos = extremaPos[[1 ;; -2 ;; 2]],
   minPos = extremaPos[[1 ;; -2 ;; 2]];
   maxPos = extremaPos[[2 ;; -2 ;; 2]]
   ];
  {minPos, maxPos}
  ]
```

Then:

```wolfram
{minPos, maxPos} = findExtremaPos[est];
```

and you can plot the minima and maxima with:

```wolfram
ListPlot[est, Joined -> True, 
 Epilog -> {
   PointSize[Large], 
   Red, Point[{#, est[[#]]} & /@ minPos], 
   Blue, Point[{#, est[[#]]} & /@ maxPos]
   },
 PlotRange -> All]
```

If you are using a newer version, `FindPeaks` can also be used directly on the smoothed data, for example:

```wolfram
peaks = FindPeaks[est, .5]
```

This supports finding peaks in a range after smoothing, but the provided material does not give a complete direct method for raw noisy data without denoising.

