The spheres and axes can be made to line up by shifting the `DiskMatrix` output by `{50,50,50}` and then compensating for the different axis conventions between `Image3D` and `Graphics3D` with a reverse plus a transpose.

A working form is:

```wl
z = 10;
p = RandomInteger[100, {z, 3}];
r = RandomInteger[10, {z}];

spheres =
  MapThread[
    Transpose[#, {3, 2, 1}] & @
      Reverse @
        RotateLeft[DiskMatrix[#1, {100, 100, 100}], #2 - {50, 50, 50}] &,
    {r, p}
  ];

i = Image3D[Plus @@ spheres, Axes -> True, AxesLabel -> {"x", "y", "z"}];

obj = GraphicsComplex[p, Sphere[Range[z], r]];
gr = Graphics3D[obj,
  Axes -> True,
  AxesLabel -> {"x", "y", "z"},
  PlotRange -> {{0, 100}, {0, 100}, {0, 100}}
];

Show[i, gr, Axes -> True]
```

So, for adding axes to the 3D concentric spheres, `Graphics3D` with `Axes -> True` and `AxesLabel -> {"x","y","z"}` is the relevant part, but if you also want them to match an `Image3D` rendering, the coordinate systems need the shift and reordering shown above.

The only supported way shown here to stop `Speak` is to let it finish the current short chunk and interrupt it at phrase boundaries. The key is checking whether Mathematica is currently speaking with:

`MathLink`CallFrontEnd[CurrentlySpeakingPacket]`

Then split the text into shorter phrases and speak them one at a time, so you can interrupt between phrases:

```mathematica
Clear[interruptibleSpeak];
interruptibleSpeak[text_String] := Module[{phrases},
  phrases = StringSplit[
    StringReplace[text, (# -> # <> "\n") & /@ {".", ";", "!", "?"}], "\n"];
  Do[
    While[MathLink`CallFrontEnd[CurrentlySpeakingPacket], Pause[0.1]];
    Speak[phrases[[i]]],
    {i, Length@phrases}
  ]
]
```

You can interrupt it in the usual way on the front end:
- Mac: Command + .
- PC: Alt + .

This does not give fully asynchronous speech; later evaluations wait until the queued phrase is spoken. It was tested on version 8.04 and reported not to work on version 7.

If your question is specifically about “Disable Input Assistant” in Mathematica 10, this does not directly address that setting; it only shows how to interrupt `Speak`.

