A workable approach is to keep the plot as the background and put the text labels in a `Graphics` layer controlled by locator positions.

The key points are:

- don’t make `myPlot` itself the locator positions
- use the plot as the back of `LocatorPane`
- keep the label positions in a separate list
- wrap only the changing graphics in `Dynamic`

A pattern like this works:

```wl
SeedRandom[1];
data = Accumulate[RandomReal[{-0.2, 0.2}, 300]];
thirds = Partition[data, 100];
interps = Interpolation /@ thirds;
myplot = ListLogLinearPlot[thirds];

DynamicModule[{pos = MapThread[{Log@#, #2[#]} &, {{5, 16, 50}, interps}]},
 LocatorPane[
  Dynamic[pos,
   (pos = MapThread[
      Function[{pt, interp},
       {#, interp@Exp@#} &@
        Clip[First@pt, Log@interp["Domain"][[1]]]
       ],
      {#, interps}
      ]) &
   ],
  Show[
   myplot,
   Graphics[{
     Dynamic@Table[
       Inset[
        Framed[
         Text@TraditionalForm[i],
         RoundingRadius -> 5,
         Background -> White
         ],
        pos[[i]]
        ],
       {i, 3}
       ]
     }]
   ],
  Appearance -> None
  ]
 ]
```

What this does:

- `myplot` is the actual plot
- `pos` stores the label positions
- `LocatorPane` lets those positions be dragged
- the `Dynamic@Table[...]` keeps the text labels attached to the current positions
- the locators themselves have no appearance, so you only see the framed labels

