The second approach works by collecting every triangle candidate from each vertex’s neighbor list, then keeping only those 3-point sets that appear exactly three times overall.

The key fix is that the naive method is wrong because from one point’s ordered neighbor list, successive pairs like {A,B}, {B,C}, {C,D} do not all necessarily form triangles with the central point P. Some of those pairs may not be adjacent in the triangulation, and even when they are, boundary cases and nontriangular local configurations cause missing or extra triangles.

A working version is:

Needs["ComputationalGeometry`"];

triangles2[points_] := Module[{tr, triples},
  tr = DelaunayTriangulation[points];
  triples = Flatten[
    Function[{v, list},
      Switch[Length[list],
        1, {},
        2, {Flatten[{v, list}]},
        _, {v, ##} & @@@ Partition[list, 2, 1, {1, 1}]
      ]
    ] @@@ tr, 1];
  Cases[GatherBy[triples, Sort], a_ /; Length[a] == 3 :> a[[1]]]
]

This returns a list of triangles as triples of point indices.

If you want to visualize them:

Graphics[
  GraphicsComplex[points, {EdgeForm[Black], FaceForm[], Polygon[triangles2[points]]}]
]

An important point: the package should be loaded before defining the function. Putting Needs["ComputationalGeometry`"] inside the function can create shadowing problems with DelaunayTriangulation.

If you want, I can also give a shorter version or explain why each triangle appears exactly three times.

