The key difference is that your middle object is not an ordinary 2×2 matrix with scalar entries; it is a 2×2 matrix whose entries are 3-element vectors. So the usual matrix product rules don’t apply directly in the scalar sense.

If you multiply on the left by {2, 4}, the result is still a vector-valued row:
{2, 4}.{{{1, 2, 3}, {3, 2, 1}}, {{2, 3, 1}, {2, 1, 3}}}
gives
{{11, 18, 13}, {17, 10, 15}}

At that point, multiplying by {6, 8} on the right is not dimensionally compatible in the same arrangement, because the 3-component vectors are sitting as entries in the rows, not aligned for the final product.

The clean way is to rearrange the tensor so the 2-vector can act on both sides consistently. In Mathematica, this is done by swapping levels 2 and 3:

Transpose[{{{1, 2, 3}, {3, 2, 1}}, {{2, 3, 1}, {2, 1, 3}}}, {1, 3, 2}]

which gives

{{{1, 3}, {2, 2}, {3, 1}}, {{2, 2}, {3, 1}, {1, 3}}}

Then the full product

{2, 4}.Transpose[{{{1, 2, 3}, {3, 2, 1}}, {{2, 3, 1}, {2, 1, 3}}}, {1, 3, 2}].{6, 8}

returns

{172, 160, 172}

So the difference in representation is really about how the tensor indices are arranged. If you want the left and right vectors to act naturally, the tensor should be permuted so its dimensions match that contraction pattern.

If your data is a sparse array adjacency matrix, convert it to a graph first, then call GraphEmbedding on that graph.

For example:

m = Import["..."];
