A practical way is to delay attaching `list1[[i]]` and `list2[[j]]` until after you’ve filtered by the scalar value. That reduces memory usage because the intermediate result is just the scalar grid, not a grid of full triples.

The suggested pattern is:

```wl
cfn[l1_, l2_] := (#.#) &[l1 - l2 + 1]

list1 = Developer`ToPackedArray[list1];
list2 = Developer`ToPackedArray[list2];

data = Outer[cfn, list1, list2, 1];
p = Position[data, x_ /; 10 < x < 15, {2}, Heads -> False];
selectdata = Transpose[
  {list1[[p[[All, 1]]]], list2[[p[[All, 2]]]], Extract[data, p]}
];
```

This does the filtering on `data`, then reconstructs the `{list1[[i]], list2[[j]], scalar}` triples only for the selected positions.

What this covers is:
- using `Outer` to compute the scalar values efficiently
- filtering by a numeric range afterward
- rebuilding the associated input vectors only for matches

If you need to avoid holding the full `data` array in memory at all, this answer does not provide a streaming solution; it only shows a lower-memory two-step approach.

