A prefix operator can’t be given a custom, low precedence like `//`, and there doesn’t appear to be a way to define a new operator that exactly matches `//`’s chaining behavior without sometimes needing parentheses.

What the thread does support is:

- custom syntax is limited;
- operator precedence cannot be modified;
- the closest built-in operator precedences to `//` are among `Colon`, `Postfix`, and `VerticalSeparator`;
- `@//` and similar custom forms can’t be defined.

So the closest guidance is that you can choose an existing operator form with similar precedence, but you can’t define a new prefix/postfix operator that will reliably behave like `//` in all chained cases without parentheses.

You can implement a simple object-oriented style in Mathematica by using symbols as types, functions as methods, and delayed definitions for method dispatch.

A minimal pattern is:

- declare a type as a symbol
- give the symbol `HoldAll`
- attach methods as definitions like `type[self_][method_] := ...`
- optionally add inheritance by forwarding missing methods to a parent type

A compact version of the approach shown is:

```mathematica
ClearAll[DeclareType];

DeclareType[s_Symbol] :=
  Function[Null,
    ClearAll[s];
    SetAttributes[s, HoldAll];
    defineMethods[s, ##];
    s,
    HoldAll];

DeclareType[s_Symbol ~ Extends ~ p_Symbol] :=
  Function[Null,
    DeclareType[s][##];
    SuperType[s] = p;
    s[self_][lhs_] := p[self][lhs];
    s,
    HoldAll];
```

Then define methods by parsing assignments passed to the type constructor:

```mathematica
ClearAll[defineMethods];
SetAttributes[defineMethods, HoldRest];
defineMethods[s_] := s;
```

This was presented as a toy implementation, but the same core idea supports the main OO features you asked for: encapsulation of behavior in methods, reuse through inheritance, and reduced coupling.

If you want, I can also help you with the next part of the implementation pattern, namely how `defineMethods` is used to attach methods and how to create instances and call methods in this style.

You can do this by setting a `FrontEndDynamicExpression` on the front end session. A working pattern is:

```wl
i = 0;
SetOptions[$FrontEndSession,
  FrontEndDynamicExpression :>
    Refresh[
      Notebooks[];
      If[GetNewNotebooks[] =!= {}, Print["New one ", ++i]],
      TrackedSymbols :> {}
    ]
]
```

The `Print[...]` part is where you put the code you want to run when a new notebook appears.

