A simple way in the same style is to use `Flatten` with a level specification if you just want to flatten nested lists, for example `Flatten[list, 1]` to flatten one level or `Flatten[list]` to flatten completely.

If you mean flattening a ragged array while preserving the submatrix sizes, the provided solution uses a more general function:

```mathematica
dynP[l_, p_] := 
   MapThread[l[[# ;; #2]] &, {{0}~Join~Most@# + 1, #} &@Accumulate@p]

ClearAll[tdim];
tdim[{dims__Integer}] := Times[dims];
tdim[dims : {__List}] := Total@Map[tdim, dims];

ClearAll[pragged];
pragged[lst_, {}] := lst;
pragged[lst_, dims : {__Integer}] := 
   Fold[Partition, lst, Most@Reverse@dims];
pragged[lst_, dims : {__List}] := 
   MapThread[pragged, {dynP[lst, tdim /@ dims], dims}];
```

