`Eigensystem` does not guarantee an “obvious” numerical order for eigenvectors; they come back in the same order as the eigenvalues, and that order is chosen for convenience of the algorithm. For symmetric/Hermitian matrices, the returned eigenvalues may be ordered by absolute value, which can look odd if you expect increasing real order.

If you want a different order, sort the eigenvalues and reorder the eigenvectors the same way. For example:

```wl
a = # + #\[Transpose] &@RandomReal[1, {10, 10}];
{ε, ψ} = Eigensystem[a];
{ε, ψ} = {ε[[#]], ψ[[#]]} &@Ordering[ε];
```

That will put the eigenvalues in increasing order, with the eigenvectors reordered accordingly.

The reason this isn’t inherently “canonical” is that for general non-Hermitian matrices eigenvalues can be complex, and there is no native total order on complex numbers.

