The approach in the thread is to use a scheduled task that checks free physical memory and calls Quit[] when it drops below your threshold.

The important point for kernel management is that RunScheduledTask is not limited to the main kernel; it can also run on subkernels if you set it there with ParallelEvaluate. The test shown was:

LaunchKernels[2];

ParallelEvaluate[
  i = 0;
  RunScheduledTask[i++, 1]
  ];

Pause[5];
ParallelEvaluate[i]

This returned {4, 4}, which shows the scheduled task was active on both subkernels.

So, for parallel computations, the closest supported guidance is:

- set up the watchdog on each kernel with ParallelEvaluate
- have the task check memory and call Quit[] when the limit is reached
- don’t rely on MemoryConstrained for tight control, since the thread says it can exceed the limit a lot

The thread does not give a more precise built-in mechanism for global memory control across machines beyond this watchdog-style approach.

