Yes, sort of.

NDSolve does not give a symbolic expression you can manipulate directly, so if you want one differential equation to depend on the numerical solution of another, you usually have to use the NDSolve result as a numerical function.

For example, if one equation has solution g[t] from NDSolve, you can define that function and then use it in a second NDSolve:

g[t_] = y[t] /. First@NDSolve[
  {y''[t] == -y[t], y[0] == 0, y'[0] == 1},
  y[t], {t, 0, 30}
];

Then use g[t] inside another equation:

y[t] /. First@NDSolve[
  {y''[t] == -2 y[t] + g[t], y[0] == 0, y'[0] == 1},
  y[t], {t, 0, 30}
]

This works as a numerical, chained computation.

What it does not do is produce a symbolic solution that you can substitute into DSolve-style algebra. If you need symbolic dependence, you would need to solve symbolically instead, or fit the numerical output to a model if you already know the expected form.

You can color the points by local density and keep the circular outline by computing a kernel density estimate and using that to assign colors to each point.

For a 2D density interpretation:

points = Map[Normalize, RandomReal[{-1, 1}, {3000, 2}]];

d = SmoothKernelDistribution[points];
colors = Hue /@ Rescale[PDF[d, #] & /@ points];

Graphics[Transpose[{colors, Point /@ points}]]

If you want to use `ListPlot`, make the points one level deeper so `PlotStyle` applies point-by-point:

ListPlot[Map[List, points], PlotStyle -> colors, AspectRatio -> Automatic]

If you want density only along the circle, use the polar angle of each point:

arc = ArcTan /@ points;
d = SmoothKernelDistribution[arc];
colors = Hue /@ Rescale[PDF[d, #] & /@ arc];

Then plot as above.

This gives you a point plot colored by distribution, rather than a filled density histogram.

Use indexed assignment with wrapped row/column ranges.

For an n×n matrix and an m×m submatrix placed with its lower-left corner at origin, you can do:

```wl
n = 8; m = 4;
origin = {6, 7};
matrix = Table[0, {n}, {n}];
sub = RandomReal[{.2, 1}, {m, m}];

{dimmat, dimsub} = Dimensions /@ {matrix, sub};

{rows, columns} = Mod[Range @@@ Transpose@{origin, origin + dimsub - 1}, dimmat, 1];

matrix[[rows, columns]] = sub;
matrix
```

This replaces the wrapped submatrix directly in `matrix`.

If you want a zero submatrix instead, just use a zero array:

```wl
