Use `h@x` rather than `h @@ x` when constructing the function.

That is:

```wl
x = {x1, x2, x3};

h[x_] := Function[Evaluate[x], x + 1 // Evaluate];
f3 = h@x;
f3 @@ x
```

This gives:

```wl
{1 + x1, 1 + x2, 1 + x3}
```

The key point is that `h@x` passes the list `x` as a single argument to `h`, so `Function[Evaluate[x], ...]` receives the variable list as intended.

If you make `standardize` have attribute `HoldFirst`, your code works as is:

```mathematica
ClearAll[standardize];
SetAttributes[standardize, HoldFirst];

standardize[a_] := Module[{alpha, beta, gamma, x},
  gamma = 2;
  For[alpha = 1, alpha <= 4, alpha++,
    For[x = 1, x <= 4, x++,
      beta = a[[alpha, x]];
      If[beta >= gamma,
        If[beta > gamma,
          a[[{gamma, beta}]] = a[[{beta, gamma}]];
          a = (a /. {beta -> gamma, gamma -> beta});
        ];
        gamma++;
      ];
    ];
  ];
  a // MatrixForm
]
```

Then calling:

```mathematica
standardize[a]
```

gives the intended result.

A way to do it is to replace the text with an overlay of the text plus a gray offset copy, rather than rasterizing it. That keeps the size increase relatively small.

The basic idea is to walk through the notebook, skip input/output cells, and rewrite the text cells like this:

```wl
nb = EvaluationNotebook[];

Module[{cell, cellObj},
  cellObj = #;
  cell = NotebookRead[cellObj];
  If[
    StringQ @ First @ cell,
    cell = ReplacePart[cell,
      1 -> Replace[cell[[1]], s_String :> BoxData @ ToBoxes @ Overlay[
        {
          s,
