The main issue in the code is a typo in the velocity definition: `Z'[t]` should be `z'[t]`.

With that fixed, the tangent component of acceleration is computed as

((acceleration[t].velocity[t])/(velocity[t].velocity[t]))*velocity[t]

and the vectors can be drawn from the point `P[t]`.

If you want the corresponding unit tangent, normal, and binormal vectors for a curve `r(t)`, the provided material only shows how to form the tangential part of acceleration for this specific parametrized curve, not the full unit T, N, and B formulas. But for this example, the corrected setup is:

```mathematica
Clear[t, x, y, z, P, velocity, acceleration, atan, tanvector];
x[t_] = 2 Sin[t];
y[t_] = 6 Sin[t/2]^2;
z[t_] = 3 Cos[t];
P[t_] = {x[t], y[t], z[t]};

curveplot = ParametricPlot3D[P[t], {t, 1, 6}, PlotStyle -> Thickness[0.01]];
velocity[t_] = {x'[t], y'[t], z'[t]};
acceleration[t_] = {x''[t], y''[t], z''[t]};

atan[t_] = ((acceleration[t].velocity[t])/(velocity[t].velocity[t]))*velocity[t];
tanvector[t_] := {Blue, Arrow[{#, # + atan[t]} &@P[t]]}

Show[curveplot, Graphics3D@Table[tanvector[t], {t, 1, 6, (6 - 1)/10}],
  PlotRange -> All, AxesLabel -> {"x", "y", "z"}]
```

So the closest supported guidance is: fix the `z` typo, and use the tangential acceleration formula as shown.

