Use a combined list of all dates, then carry the last known price forward for each series.

A simple way in Mathematica is:

```wolfram
fillForCompare[data1_, data2_] :=
 Module[{f1, f2, all, last},
   all = Union @@ {data1, data2}[[All, All, 1]];
   (f1[#] := last = #2) & @@@ data1;
   (f2[#] := last = #2) & @@@ data2;
   f1[_] := last; 
   f2[_] := last;
   {all, f1 /@ all, f2 /@ all}\[Transpose]
 ]
```

For your example:

```wolfram
list1 = {{{2007, 1, 2}, 12.04}, {{2007, 1, 4}, 11.51}, {{2007, 1, 5}, 12.14}};
list2 = {{{2007, 1, 2}, 20.91}, {{2007, 1, 3}, 24.34}, {{2007, 1, 4}, 24.67}, {{2007, 1, 5}, 20.97}};

fillForCompare[list1, list2]
```

This gives date-aligned output with the missing price in `list1` filled from the previous available date:

```wolfram
{
 {{2007, 1, 2}, 12.04, 20.91},
 {{2007, 1, 3}, 12.04, 24.34},
 {{2007, 1, 4}, 11.51, 24.67},
 {{2007, 1, 5}, 12.14, 20.97}
}
```

If you want the two series returned separately but aligned to the same dates, this version does that too:

```wolfram
fillForCompare2[data1_, data2_, fill_: Missing[]] :=
 Module[{f1, f2, all, last = fill},
   all = Union @@ {data1, data2}[[All, All, 1]];
   (f1[#] := last = #2) & @@@ data1;
   (f2[#] := last = #2) & @@@ data2;
   f1[_] := last; 
   f2[_] := last;
   Transpose /@ {{all, f1 /@ all}, last = fill; {all, f2 /@ all}}
 ]
```

This is the closest direct solution supported by the provided material.

