You can use a monotone cubic Hermite interpolant, which keeps the CDF monotone while remaining differentiable almost everywhere, and then differentiate it to get a PDF.

A compact way to build it is:

```mathematica
fcint[data_] := Module[{del, slopes, tau},
  del = #2/#1 & @@@ Differences[data];
  slopes = Flatten[{del[[1]], MovingAverage[del, 2], del[[-1]]}];
  tau = MapThread[
    Min,
    Through[{Append, Prepend}[Min[#, 1] & /@
      (3 del/(Norm /@ Partition[slopes, 2, 1])), 1]]
  ];
  Interpolation[
    Transpose[{List /@ data[[All, 1]], data[[All, -1]], slopes tau}],
    InterpolationOrder -> 3, Method -> "Hermite"
  ]
]
```

Then:

```mathematica
fun = fcint[data];
pdf = D[fun[x], x];
```

This gives you an interpolated CDF that is monotone, and a corresponding PDF from its derivative.

If your goal is specifically a smooth, nonparametric CDF estimate from sampled cumulative probabilities, this is the closest supported approach in the material provided. It also directly addresses the issue that first-order interpolation is monotone but not differentiable, while higher-order interpolation can be made monotone by using Hermite interpolation with slope limiting.

