Use a compiled routine that scans the points once and keeps only those whose distance from the last kept point is greater than `delta`.

A version matching the solution shown is:

```wl
getDistantPoints = 
  Compile[{{pts, _Real, 2}, {delta, _Real}},
    Module[{res = Table[{0., 0.}, {Length[pts]}], ctr = 1},
      res[[1]] = pts[[1]];
      Do[
        If[Norm[pts[[i]] - res[[ctr]]] > delta,
          res[[++ctr]] = pts[[i]]
        ],
        {i, Length[pts]}
      ];
      Take[res, ctr]
    ],
    CompilationTarget -> "C",
    RuntimeOptions -> "Speed"
  ];
```

Then apply it inside your nested structure:

```wl
Clear[GZFastAlt];
GZFastAlt[delta_, data_] :=
  Module[{ldata = data},
    ParallelTable[
      Table[
        getDistantPoints[ldata[[subNO, dispNo, All, ;; 2]], delta],
        {dispNo, Range[Length[ldata[[subNO]]]]}
      ],
      {subNO, Range[5]}
    ]
  ];
```

This was reported to produce the same results as the original approach on smaller tests, and to run in about 2 seconds on a 6-core machine.

It also keeps the data packed, which is important for memory use with large datasets.

If you want, I can also show a non-compiled pure-Wolfram Language version of the same logic.

If your data already contains the sampled surface, the usual approach is to stop `ListPlot3D` from connecting points across the discontinuity.

Two supported ways from the example are:

- Post-process the graphics and remove polygons that are too steep.
- Use `RegionFunction` to exclude the locations of the branch cut.

A simple polygon-filtering idea is:

```wl
data = With[{g := GaussianFilter[RandomReal[{-1, 1}, {50, 50}], 25]}, ArcTan[g, g]];
plot = ListPlot3D[data, Mesh -> None] // Normal;

toosteep[Polygon[pts_, ___]] := Module[{z = pts[[All, 3]]},
  Max[z] - Min[z] > 1
];

DeleteCases[plot, _Polygon?toosteep, -1]
```

If you can identify the discontinuity region, you can exclude it with `RegionFunction`:

```wl
exclude = Image[data] ~GradientFilter~ 1 ~Binarize~ 1.5 ~Dilation~ 1;

regionfunc = ListInterpolation[ImageData@exclude, InterpolationOrder -> 0];

ListPlot3D[data, Mesh -> None,
  RegionFunction -> Function[{x, y, z}, regionfunc[y, x] < 1]
]
```

So the closest guidance is: use either steep-polygon deletion after plotting, or a `RegionFunction` based on a mask of the branch cut region.

