LibraryLink does not take ownership of a returned string, so it won’t free that memory for you. The supported pattern is to keep the allocated string alive until Wolfram Language has read it, and then free it in a later call.

So yes, in the general case you need some separate cleanup step, but it does not have to be a separate publicly exposed function. A common approach is to free the previous string the next time your library function is called, for example:

```cpp
if (str != NULL) {
  delete str;
}
str = new std::string("some string to return");
```

This covers the case where you return dynamically allocated strings. If the string is fixed and does not need freeing, you can return it without allocating.

Your function is using K, which is a built-in symbolic name in Mathematica. That conflicts with your own kernel function.

So this:

K[t_] := 1.078222*Exp[-(t^2/0.273800)];

can cause problems inside your smoothing code. The fix is to use a different name, like k:

k[t_] := 1.078222*Exp[-(t^2/0.273800)];

Then your function should work.

Also, in your example, the empty graphic comes from the symbol conflict, not from ParallelTable itself. After renaming K to k, this version is fine:

KSmoothTest[vy_, b_] := Block[{n, weights},
  n = Length[vy];
  weights = Table[k[i/b], {i, 0, Length[vy] - 1}];
  Table[
    Sum[weights[[Abs[i - j] + 1]]*vy[[j]], {j, 1, n}]/
    Sum[weights[[Abs[i - j] + 1]], {j, 1, n}],
    {i, 1, n}
  ]
];

If you want, I can also point out a couple of other issues in the code that may affect the result.

