Yes. The closest working approach is to treat the 2D image as a radial profile, sample it with an interpolating function, and build a 3D array by evaluating that profile at the radius `Sqrt[x^2 + y^2]`. Then render the result as a volumetric image.

A basic version looks like this:

```wl
source = Import["https://i.sstatic.net/W76CQ.jpg"];

(* reflect so the rotation axis is at the top edge *)
img = ColorNegate @ ImageReflect[source, Top -> Bottom];

len = Divide @@ N @ ImageDimensions[img];

(* one interpolating function per color channel *)
{r, g, b} = ListInterpolation[#, {{0, 1}, {0, len}}] & /@ ImageData /@ ColorSeparate[img];

step = 0.02;

data = Table[
  With[{rad = Sqrt[x^2 + y^2]},
    If[rad > 1, 0, g[rad, z]]
  ],
  {x, -1, 1, step},
  {y, -1, 1, step},
  {z, 0, len, step}
];

Image3D[data,
  ClipRange -> {All, {0, 1/step}, {0, 1/step}},
  ColorFunction -> "RainbowOpacity"
]
```

This creates a 3D volume by revolving the image around the axis, and `Image3D` displays it.

A few important notes from the working example:

- If the image has color information that matters, it’s better to separate the grayscale/opacity component from the colored overlay and process them separately.
- The example above uses only one channel (`g`) for simplicity.
- To retain the original colors, you would generate the 3D volume for each channel and then combine them with `ColorCombine`.
- To make a cut-away, you can remove part of the generated 3D data before display, for example by zeroing out one quadrant or using a clipped view.

So the supported method is: reflect the image, interpolate its pixel values, revolve those values by evaluating at radius, and then visualize the resulting 3D data.

