The square-root version is slow because Mathematica is doing a full symbolic exact expansion, and that can explode in complexity: the example leads to millions of terms/sequences, with repeated checks to see whether products of radicals simplify exactly.

If you only want speed and can tolerate numerical approximation, converting the radicals to floating-point first makes it much faster:

Expand[N[Sqrt[2] + Sqrt[3] + Sqrt[5] + Sqrt[6] + Sqrt[7]]^100];

That runs essentially instantly.

If you want an exact result but faster than expanding everything at once, a faster approach is to expand incrementally, one multiplication at a time:

term = Sqrt[2] + Sqrt[3] + Sqrt[5] + Sqrt[6] + Sqrt[7];
Nest[Expand[# term] &, term, 100 - 1];

That avoids some repeated work and is much faster than the single huge Expand call.

