A direct way is to factor the binomial coefficient, then split the prime powers by whether the prime is below or above k.

For example:

```mathematica
dec[n_, k_] := Module[{num, u},
  num = Binomial[n, k];
  u = Times @@ (#1^#2 & @@@ Cases[FactorInteger[num], {p_ /; p < k, e_}]);
  {u, num/u}
]
```

This returns `{U, V}` where `U` is the product of prime powers with primes less than k, and `V` is the remaining factor.

Example:

```mathematica
dec[17, 5]
```

If you want to match the interval exactly as in your description, using primes `p <= k` for `U` and `k < p <= n` for `V`, then this version is closer:

```mathematica
dec[n_, k_] := Module[{num, u, v},
  num = Binomial[n, k];
  u = Times @@ (#1^#2 & @@@ Cases[FactorInteger[num], {p_ /; p <= k, e_}]);
  v = Times @@ (#1^#2 & @@@ Cases[FactorInteger[num], {p_ /; k < p <= n, e_}]);
  {u, v}
]
```

Your original attempt did not work because it only checked whether `p` divides `Binomial[n, k]`; it did not extract the full prime-power factorization. The factorization step is needed to get the exponents.

