In Mathematica, the standard way to make variables local inside a module is to use Module[...].

Example:

Module[{x, y},
  x = 3;
  y = x^2;
  {x, y}
]

Here x and y are local to the Module and do not affect variables with the same names outside it.

If you want a function where arguments are local automatically, a typical pattern is:

f[a_] := Module[{x, y},
  x = a + 1;
  y = x^2;
  y
]

There is no global setting to make all variables local by default everywhere in a notebook or module; you normally have to introduce local variables explicitly with Module, Block, or With depending on the behavior you want.

If you want, I can also show the difference between Module, Block, and With with working examples.

