A workable way to do this is to temporarily replace subscripted symbols by ordinary symbols, run `FullSimplify`, then convert back.

The function below does that:

```mathematica
fullSimplify[expr_] :=
 Module[
  {uqString, counter, definedQ, toMapper, backRules, symb, simpExpr, bla},

  backRules = {};
  definedQ[x__] := False;
  uqString = ToString[Unique[bla]];
  counter = 0;

  Block[{Subscript},
   Subscript[x__] :=
    (
     If[definedQ[x],
      toMapper[x],
      definedQ[x] = True;
      symb = Symbol[uqString <> ToString[counter]];
      counter++;
      AppendTo[backRules, symb :> Subscript[x]];
      toMapper[x] = symb
      ]
     );
   simpExpr = FullSimplify[expr];
   ];

  Remove[toMapper, definedQ];
  simpExpr /. backRules
  ]
```

Then you can use:

```mathematica
fullSimplify[f1] /. rule1
```

This should give the simplified form with your original subscripted variables restored.

One caveat is that the temporary symbols created inside `Module` need to be cleaned up, and using a linked list would be better than repeated `AppendTo`.

