The difference is that Block is dynamically scoped, while Module is lexically scoped.

In the example, Block[{Print = ...}, ...] temporarily changes the global meaning of Print for code executed inside the Block, including functions called from it. But if you refer to Print again inside that same Block definition, it will also see the localized meaning, which causes recursion unless you arrange to preserve the original definition separately.

Module does not do that kind of dynamic rebinding. It creates new local symbols, so it’s useful for lexical locals, not for temporarily overriding a global symbol name throughout a call chain.

For your specific need, the closest supported approach is to use Internal`InheritedBlock to localize the redefinition while preserving the existing definitions, and then use a Module plus a guard variable to avoid recursion. The accepted pattern was:

Internal`InheritedBlock[{Print},
    Unprotect[Print];
    Module[{inPrint},
       Print[arg_]/;!TrueQ[inPrint]:=
          Block[{inPrint = True},
              (CopyToClipboard[#]; Print[#])&[arg]
          ];
    ];
    your-code
]

So in short: Block changes meanings dynamically; Module creates fresh local names.

