It’s a precision issue.

With machine-precision input, NSolve may miss a real root or return slightly different approximations, while FindRoot can still converge to the real solution if started near it.

If you rewrite the parameters with exact values, NSolve finds the real root too:

Clear[k, sol1, a, G, j, l, equ];
a = 3/10; 
G = 4;
equ = k^(G (1 - a)) == (1 - a)/G Sum[(G - j + 1) a^(j - 1) k^((G - j) (1 - a)), {j, G}];

Solve[equ, k] // N
NSolve[equ, k, WorkingPrecision -> 20]
sol1 = FindRoot[equ, {k, 1}]

The exact/extended-precision forms give the real solution as well, alongside the complex ones.

So the behavior you saw is not that FindRoot is “better” in general; it’s that NSolve with machine-precision data can be more sensitive. Using exact numbers or increasing WorkingPrecision is the closest fix supported here.

