This sounds like the `Manipulate` is doing too much implicit updating, and the `Dynamic[MatrixForm[r]]` / `Show[...]` parts are getting tangled up with the simulation state.

The guidance supported by the example is to simplify the dynamics:

- use a `Trigger` control for the time step,
- restrict updating with `TrackedSymbols :> {dt}`,
- keep the simulation variables local to the `Manipulate` rather than global,
- and, if needed, put the update function in `Initialization`.

That approach makes the time evolution explicit and avoids the kind of intermittent stopping you’re seeing.

A version of the pattern that was reported to work is:

```mathematica
Manipulate[
 {r, v, a} = Step[r, v, a, dt];

 Grid[{
   {Show[{Graphics@Circle[{0, 0}, 1],
      Graphics[{PointSize[Large], Red, Point[r]}]}]},
   {r, Norm@r}
   }
  ],

 Control[{{dt, 0.005, "dt:"}, 0.00001, 1, 0.00001,
   ControlType -> Trigger, DisplayAllSteps -> True,
   ImageSize -> Small, AnimationRate -> Automatic}],

 {{r, {1, 0}}, None},
 {{v, {0, 1}}, None},
 {{a, {-1, 0}}, None},
 TrackedSymbols :> {dt},
 Initialization :>
  {
   Step[r_, v_, a_, dt_] := Block[{nr, nv, na},
     nv = v + dt/2 a;
     nr = r + dt nv;
     na = -nr;
     nv = nv + dt/2 a;
     {nr, nv, na}
     ]
   }
 ]
```

So the closest supported advice is: don’t let `Dynamic` update freely in this setup; drive the simulation with a `Trigger` and limit what `Manipulate` tracks. That is the fix suggested by the working example.

A close way to get this behavior is to build the scoping wrapper with `Block` or by creating a local context, rather than trying to change Mathematica’s default scoping globally.

One workable definition is:

```wl
ClearAll[Scope];
SetAttributes[Scope, HoldAll];

Scope[{globals : (_Symbol | _Set | _SetDelayed) ...}, body_] :=
  Internal`InheritedBlock[{$ContextPath},
    globals;
    Module[{context, localbody = MakeBoxes[body]},
      Internal`WithLocalSettings[
        BeginPackage[SymbolName@context <> "`"],
        ReleaseHold@MakeExpression@localbody,
        EndPackage[]
      ]
    ]
  ]
```

With this, you can do things like:

```wl
x = 1;
y = 1;

Scope[{x = 2},
  y = 2;
  a = 0;
  {x, y, a}
]
```

which gives the intended effect inside the scope: `x` is treated as global because you listed it, while `y` and `a` are local to the scope.

The same source also notes a simpler alternative based on `Block`, which is likely closer to the intended behavior, though the full helper definitions are needed for that version.

One caveat: the `BeginPackage`/context-based version may generate shadowing messages when symbols are first created globally and then made local.

If you want, I can also help adapt this into a cleaner `Block`-based `Scope` definition.

Using `Module` here won’t give you a value that can be updated interactively inside `ToBoxes`/`MakeBoxes`. The working approach supported by the thread is to use `DynamicModule` instead:

```wl
SetAttributes[AutoInputField, HoldAllComplete];

AutoInputField /: MakeBoxes[
  AutoInputField[list_: {}, displayNum_: 3, imageSize_: 100], form_
] :=
  ToBoxes @ DynamicModule[{x = "default"},
    x = "default";
    Column[{
      InputField[Dynamic@x, String, ContinuousAction -> True, ImageSize -> imageSize],
      Dynamic@x
