The problem isn’t that Mathematica can’t minimize at all; it’s that this objective is very strongly multimodal, with many deceptive local minima. Nelder-Mead is one of the methods that performs worst on this kind of landscape, so changing its ratios or random seed usually won’t fix the core issue.

The closest guidance supported here is:

- try a different global method, especially differential evolution (`Method -> "DifferentialEvolution"` in `NMinimize`);
- if you stay with Nelder-Mead, run it many times from many different initial simplices, because a single start is unlikely to find a good basin;
- if you repeatedly minimize the same function, a compiled implementation may help with speed, though that does not change the difficulty of the optimization itself.

So the practical answer is that Mathematica can do this, but not reliably with Nelder-Mead alone on this kind of problem. The function’s structure is the main obstacle, not the software.

