Yes, for a single-variable differentiable function, a useful approach is to “track” the function with NDSolve and detect zero crossings with events.

In the example, you define

f[x_] := BesselJ[1, x]^2 + BesselK[1, x]^2 - Sin[Sin[x]]

and then solve an auxiliary ODE

y'[x] == D[f[x], x]

with an initial condition like y[10] == f[10], integrating backward over the interval of interest. Using event detection, you can collect points where y[x] hits 0, which correspond to roots of f[x].

A direct form shown is:

zeros = Reap[
  y[x] /. First[
    NDSolve[
      {
        y'[x] == Evaluate[D[f[x], x]],
        y[10] == f[10]
      },
      y[x], {x, 10, 0},
      Method -> {"EventLocator", "Event" -> y[x],
        "EventAction" :> Sow[{x, y[x]}]}
    ]
  ]
][[2, 1]]

This produces a list of approximate roots in the range.

A more modern version uses WhenEvent:

zeros = Reap[
  NDSolve[
    {
      y'[x] == D[f[x], x],
      y[10] == f[10],
      WhenEvent[y[x] == 0, Sow[{x, y[x]}]]
    },
    y, {x, 10, 0}
  ]
][[2, 1]]

This idea is most useful when the function is differentiable and can be tracked numerically. It is not a general guaranteed “all roots at once” symbolic method, but it can be effective when Reduce is too slow or cannot handle the equation.

For your contour-plot idea, this same approach suggests evaluating the roots along a parameter slice such as a == 0 by solving the one-dimensional equation obtained from that slice and collecting the zero crossings over the x-interval.

Nothing is wrong with setting one list to another by itself. In your example, the issue is that list1 and list2 initially refer to the same list, so changing elements through either name affects that same underlying list unless you create an independent copy first.

The bigger problem in the example, though, is with Play: it has HoldAll, so the expression involving i is not evaluated when you build the list. As a result, i is effectively left symbolic until Play later assigns numerical values to t, and then all entries can behave as if they use the current i.

The supported fix is to force evaluation of the expression before passing it to Play:

list1 = {0, 0, 0};
list2 = list1;
Do[
  list1[[i]] = Play[Evaluate @ Cos[150*i*2*Pi*t], {t, 0, 0.5}];
  list2[[i]] = Plot[Cos[i*2*Pi*t], {t, 0, 0.5}];
  ,
  {i, 3}
]

So, the closest guidance is:
- assigning one list to another does not make a separate copy in this case;
- Play’s HoldAll behavior is what causes i not to be evaluated as expected;
- wrapping the expression in Evaluate is the suggested fix.

