Use the same pattern: sort by the column you want to restrict on, accumulate that column, then take items until the cutoff is reached.

A general version is:

takeParetoFromList[data_, cut_: 0.8, paretoColumn_: 1] :=
 Module[{dataSort, total, elements},
  dataSort = Reverse@SortBy[data, #[[paretoColumn]] &];
  total = Total[data[[All, paretoColumn]]];
  elements = LengthWhile[(Accumulate@dataSort[[All, paretoColumn]])/total, # <= cut &];
  Take[dataSort, elements]
 ]

Example:

takeParetoFromList[list, 0.8, 2]

This returns the items whose values in column 2 account for up to 80% of the total, after sorting from largest to smallest.

If you need to “cut” elements based on a different restriction function, the closest supported approach here is to replace the chosen column with the values produced by that function, then apply the same sort/accumulate/take logic.

You can’t use patterns like `_` in $Assumptions and expect Mathematica to apply them globally. The assumption system is not pattern-based.

What does work is setting $Assumptions to a concrete assumption expression, for example:

$Assumptions = x > 0 && Element[x, Reals];

Even then, this only affects functions that actually consult the Assumptions option. For those functions, if you don’t give an explicit Assumptions value, they will use Assumptions :> $Assumptions.

So the closest guidance is:
- use an actual logical assumption expression in $Assumptions, not patterns;
- only some functions will pay attention to it;
- if you need broader behavior, you’d need a more advanced mechanism like $NewSymbol, but that still won’t make every function obey assumptions automatically.

