The supported faster approach is to avoid using Table together with If inside nested loops. The faster method shown is to sort once and then accumulate the values, rather than repeatedly testing conditions.

For the matrix generation part, this is faster:

weights = RandomReal[{-10, 10}, {bNum, nNum}];
newMat  = RandomReal[{-10, 10}, {bNum, nNum}];

For the main computation, the faster pattern shown is a function like this:

process2[x_, w_, {y0_, dy_, ny_}] :=
  Module[{x0, w0},
    x0 = Ceiling[(x - y0)/dy] ~Clip~ {0, ny};
    x0 = Join[{0}, Sort @ x0, {ny}];
    w0 = {0} ~Join~ Accumulate[w[[Ordering @ x]]];
    Join @@ MapThread[ConstantArray, {w0, Differences @ x0}]
  ]

And then use it row by row:

MapThread[process2[#1, #2, {-10, 0.1, 201}] &, {newMat, weights}]

That is the closest faster method supported here, and it was reported to be much faster than the original Table/If version. If you want, I can help adapt your exact code into this form.

